<article class="article">
  <div class="eyebrow">nanogpt-served · llm inference</div>
  <h1 class="post-title">Continuous batching from scratch</h1>
  <p class="post-sub">How iteration-level scheduling makes LLM serving fast — built by hand,
  then measured: <b>static 10 steps / 65% utilization → continuous 6 steps / 100%</b>,
  same requests served.</p>
  <div class="post-meta">Balaji Chidambaram · June 2026 · ~6 min read</div>

<p>I've worked with LLM APIs a lot over the years, but I've always been fascinated
by how everything works under the hood. Since undergrad I've been drawn to GPUs and
distributed systems — one of my projects was large-scale processing with Hadoop and
MapReduce — but I never had the resources to go deep, since access to serious GPUs
was expensive back in 2020 (it's even more expensive now 🤣).</p>

<p>Working with LLM APIs day-to-day now — Vertex AI, OpenAI, Claude — that old
curiosity came back: how do these services serve live, millisecond inference for
massive models at scale? I want to pivot my career toward inference and platform
engineering, and this blog — starting with <code class="inl">nanogpt-served</code> —
is me learning it by building it from scratch.</p>

<p>When a server runs one model for many users, the requests arrive and finish at
<em>different</em> times. The hard question isn't <em>which output belongs to which
request</em> (that's solved by batch <b>row</b>) — it's <b>when do you decide the
batch?</b> That single timing choice is the gap between a slow server and a fast one.</p>

<h2><span class="n">01</span>Static batching — the slow default</h2>
<p>Grab a batch, run it in <b>lockstep</b>, everyone leaves when the <b>slowest</b>
finishes. Five requests needing <code class="inl">[2, 6, 3, 4, 2]</code> tokens,
3 GPU lanes:</p>
<div class="panel">
  <div class="scroll"><svg id="static" role="img" aria-label="Static batching timeline"></svg></div>
  <div class="legend">
    <span><i class="sw req"></i>request decoded (useful)</span>
    <span><i class="sw idle"></i>idle lane — finished request, dead weight</span>
    <span><i class="sw none"></i>no lane (batch smaller than max)</span>
  </div>
  <div class="cap">Two wastes: <b>idle lanes</b> (R1 done at step 2 but still
  decoded through step 6) and the <b>wave barrier</b> — R4/R5 can't start until
  <em>all</em> of wave 1 drains, even though lanes sat free from step 3.</div>
</div>

<h2><span class="n">02</span>Continuous batching — re-decide every step</h2>
<p>vLLM's term is <b>iteration-level scheduling</b>: the scheduler runs between
<em>every</em> decode step. A finished request leaves immediately; a queued one
takes the freed lane next step.</p>
<div class="panel">
  <div class="scroll"><svg id="cont" role="img" aria-label="Continuous batching timeline"></svg></div>
  <div class="legend">
    <span><i class="sw req"></i>request decoded (useful)</span>
    <span><i class="sw none"></i>no lane (batch shrank — <b>not</b> wasted)</span>
  </div>
  <div class="cap">Watch <b>lane A</b>: R1 (blue) finishes at step 2, R4 (purple)
  takes its lane at step 3 — no barrier, no wait. The only gap (lane C, step 6)
  is the unavoidable <b>drain tail</b>: the batch shrinks to 2, it does
  <em>not</em> decode dead weight. That's why util reads 100%, not 94%.</div>
</div>

<h2><span class="n">03</span>The scoreboard</h2>
<p>A <b>slot-step</b> = one GPU lane used for one decode step — the atomic unit of
work. <code class="inl">total</code> = lanes you paid for; <code class="inl">useful</code>
= lanes that produced a token a request still wanted. Same model, same prompts,
same 5 requests served:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>mode</th><th>steps</th><th>useful / total<br>slot-steps</th><th>utilization</th><th>served</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>static</td><td>10</td><td>17 / 26</td><td>65%</td><td>5</td></tr>
    <tr class="win"><td>continuous</td><td><span class="delta">6</span></td><td>17 / 17</td><td><span class="delta">100%</span></td><td>5</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<div class="keypt">
  <b>Identical work, identical output.</b> Continuous batching didn't skip
  anything — <code class="inl">served</code> and <code class="inl">useful</code>
  are unchanged. It just stopped wasting lanes: <b>26→17</b> slot-steps (no idle
  lanes) and <b>10→6</b> steps (no head-of-line wait). On a real GPU those two
  numbers are throughput&nbsp;↑ and time-to-first-token&nbsp;↓.
</div>
<div class="keypt">
  <b>Why useful == total in continuous?</b> You <code class="inl">evict</code> at
  the end of every step, so at decode time every lane holds a request that still
  wants a token. No lane is ever spent on a finished request. Static can't evict
  mid-wave (it's lockstep), so the gap is pure waste.
</div>

<h2><span class="n">04</span>Policy vs mechanism — the vLLM shape</h2>
<p>The build accidentally drew the real architecture boundary. The
<b>scheduler</b> decides batch membership each iteration (policy); the
<b>decode</b> just runs a forward pass (mechanism) and is oblivious to <em>which</em>
requests it holds.</p>
<div class="panel scroll"><svg id="arch" role="img" aria-label="Policy over mechanism"></svg>
  <div class="cap">This is vLLM in miniature: a <code class="inl">Scheduler</code>
  over a model-execution layer. The missing piece between this toy and vLLM is
  inside the mechanism box.</div>
</div>

<h2><span class="n">05</span>The core decision: admit → decode → evict</h2>
<p>The scaffolding (request queue, the decode pass) is plumbing. The whole idea
lives in the <b>ordering</b> of the step loop — admit waiting requests into free
lanes, decode the current batch once, then evict whoever finished so their lane
opens up <em>next</em> step:</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># ADMIT: fill every free lane (while, not if).</span>
while len(self.running) &lt; self.max_batch_size and self.waiting:
    self.running.append(self.waiting.pop(0))

<span class="c"># DECODE: one forward over the current batch.</span>
self._decode(self.running)
self.steps += 1
self.total_slot_steps  += len(self.running)   <span class="c"># cost</span>
self.useful_slot_steps += len(self.running)   <span class="c"># all useful → evict-at-end</span>

<span class="c"># EVICT: finished requests leave, freeing their lane for next step.</span>
done = [r for r in self.running if r.done]
self.finished.extend(done)
self.running = [r for r in self.running if not r.done]</code></pre>

<h2><span class="n">06</span>What's next — why a KV cache doesn't just drop in</h2>
<div class="next">
  <b>The deliberate cheat:</b> <code class="inl">_decode</code> <b>recomputes</b>
  the full context every step — no KV cache. A naive single-sequence KV cache
  assumes <b>one sequence of one length</b>; a batch whose membership and lengths
  change every step shatters that single contiguous tensor. That exact mismatch is
  what <b>PagedAttention</b> solves — a cache manager that hands out fixed-size
  blocks so ragged, churning sequences share GPU memory without copying. That's the
  next build.
</div>

<p>Building this drove home something I hadn't appreciated: inference isn't only
about the GPU — it's just as much about the framework and software layered above it,
from tensors up to the scheduler. The batch scheduler packages requests together so
the GPU's parallel compute actually gets used instead of sitting idle. LLM inference
used to be a black box to me — I always wondered why inference APIs charge less per
token for batch requests and cached prompts. Now I understand the machinery behind it.</p>

<p>Full code: <a href="https://github.com/balachidam21/nanogpt-served">github.com/balachidam21/nanogpt-served</a> — feedback welcome.</p>
